A||N||13||tiacPaul||Engine Sputter/hesitation||||13||
Z||000000||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||06:07 PM||||I put a new duke in my Fiero a few months ago and have had no problems until a few weeks ago when it started sputtering.  I have tried fuel system cleaners, reprograming the computer, fuel filter, sensors galor, but the problem remains.  I need help before i spend any more $!||||reg||13
Z||000001||fierospeeder||02-01-2000||06:18 PM||||does it hesitate and sputter while your accelerating?<P>do you have brand new sparkplug wires?||||reg||1
Z||000002||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||07:03 PM||||Yes, only while accelerating. And it only seams to do it when warm.  New plugs, wires, ignition coil, tuned, distributer cap looks good...<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000004||william_thorniii||02-01-2000||07:11 PM||||If you have a DIS Duke don't use Bosch Platinums.  They don't get along.  Bosch Platinums do work very well for the standard Duke.<P>Also has the climate changed drastically since you noticed this problem?  My Duke has always acted up when it is wet and or cold.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||02-01-2000||07:22 PM||||unplug the tach filter and make sure it's not part of the problem.<P>if you still have the old coil put it back on. the new one could be bad. pull the plugs and check them. make sure they are gapped correctly. Don't use non spec gap on sparkplugs. over gapping heats up the coil.<BR>under gaped won't fire hot enough.<P>take off the dist module, clean it, and install it with new heat sink compound(available at radio shack). A dirty/loose module usually won't work right.<P>check the rotor button in the dist cap and the rotor center. signs of arcing on these contacts mean the conection is floating. The parts are defective and must be replaced.<P>re shoot static timing. if it's off even a couple degrees the ECM will start crabbing about it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||08:42 PM||||They're champion plugs and look good.  I checked the gaps already and they were fine.  The new coil was put on to fix the problem and changed nothing.  And like i said the distributer looks fine.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||02-01-2000||09:25 PM||||Double check the wires by running it at night and look for arcing.  Some low quality wires are not sheilded well enough and arc at the looms.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000008||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||10:31 PM||||What is a "tach filter" and a "dist module"?<BR>the o2 sensor is also new.||||reg||5
Z||000009||shaolin||02-02-2000||11:15 PM||||Just out a suggestion. Have you maybe blown a head gasket? That is what my Porsche and my Isuzu both done. That could be your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000010||tiacPaul||02-03-2000||10:35 PM||||If the head gasket were blown the car wouldn't run!  That's why I replaced the engine in the first place!<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000011||Phaeton||02-03-2000||11:01 PM||||I've never been near a DIS Duke, do they have a knock sensor?||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||02-04-2000||12:13 AM||||Better check out the tach filter like Ogre says. If you need more info about this, do a search, there must be a dozen topics discussing this, it's a common Fiero problem. Also, the DIS dukes have a habit of coils going bad.||||reg||1
Z||000013||overlord||02-04-2000||08:58 AM||||whats a DIS duke?<P>im curious because i have a duke and just bought bosche platinums...||||reg||1
Z||000014||DRH||02-04-2000||12:58 PM||||DIS was used on 87 and 88 4 cyl. There is no Distributor. Plugs are fired directly from 2 coil packs. The coils are fired electronically from a crankshaft sensor. <P>Since it is running from the crankshaft it does not know which of the 2 cylinders that are approaching the top of their stroke to fire. So it fires both (actually the cylinder on the exhaust stroke may spark "backwards" ???). Called "Waste Spark" or something like that. I think this is why the Platinums are not recommended for this engine.<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 02-04-2000).]||||reg||1
